>> +  When Fastmail rejects a message, the client gets Fastmail's code
>> +  and text. When Fastmail cannot be reached, the client gets a 4xx
>> +  reply. smtprelay never accepts a message it has not handed to
>> +  Fastmail, and never sends a bounce.
>
> Interesting, so this is the tradeoff I guess? With a queueing server, I
> can deliver emails from the client offline.

Yes, that is the tradeoff. A queue answers 250 to everything it can
hold, so it is also what hides a later rejection from the sender. The
relay keeps the failure in the client's session, and the cost is that
nothing holds mail while Fastmail is down. I will state that cost in
the commit message.

> How does this scenario work when I can't reach the remote server? I
> anticipate notmuch-emacs will be my primary client for working with this
> system.

smtprelay answers 4xx and msmtp exits non-zero. Emacs then does what
it does for any failed send. I checked message.el in Emacs 31.1 and
notmuch-mua.el on this host:

- message-send-mail-with-sendmail pops up the " sendmail errors"
  buffer with msmtp's output, and signals "Sending...failed".
- The error leaves message-send before it reaches message-do-fcc, so
  no sent copy is written, and the compose buffer stays open with your
  text in it.
- To send later, either leave the buffer open and press C-c C-c again,
  or press C-c C-p (notmuch-draft-postpone). That saves the message in
  notmuch tagged draft; resume it from the message with "e" and send.

So nothing is lost, but nothing retries for you either. If you want
mail written offline to go out by itself, the queue has to live in
your client, not in lab-mail. msmtp ships msmtpq, a wrapper that
queues locally when the network is down and sends the queue later.
I have not checked how it treats a 5xx. It must not queue a permanent
rejection, or it brings back the problem the relay solves.

One more thing to know. lab-mail listens on beastie's loopback only,
so your Emacs must run on beastie, or in a jail on it, to send through
lab-mail at all. A laptop that is offline cannot reach lab-mail
whether it queues or not.

Questions:

1. Where will your notmuch-emacs run: on beastie, in a jail on
   beastie, or on another machine? If another machine, the access
   section has to change.

2. When Fastmail is down, is it enough for your client to keep the
   draft and let you send again by hand, or do you want a client-side
   queue? If a queue, should your client and its queue be in this
   spec, next to lab-claude?

>> +  server rejects any recipient, msmtp sends no DATA and exits non-zero
>> +  with the server's reply, so the whole message is abandoned.
>
> How does notmuch-email respond in this case?

The same way as above. msmtp exits non-zero with smtprelay's reply,
for example the 5xx for someone@example.com. Emacs shows it in the
" sendmail errors" buffer, signals the error, writes no sent copy,
and leaves the buffer open. You fix the recipient and press C-c C-c.
No recipient gets the message, because msmtp never sent DATA.

There is one wrinkle. notmuch tags the message you are replying to
with notmuch-message-replied-tags ("+replied" by default) from
message-send-hook. That hook runs before the send, so a failed reply
still marks the original replied. If you add "-inbox" to that list,
a failed reply also drops the original from your inbox. That is a
notmuch-emacs behavior, not something lab-mail can fix.
